# Rendered by godogen publish.sh — not source
.claude/
CLAUDE.md
.agents/
AGENTS.md
.codex/
godot.md

# Godot import cache and .NET build output
.godot/
bin/
obj/

# Exported builds. Reproducible from export_presets.cfg with one command, and
# an .exe plus its .pck is sixty megabytes of derived file per export.
build/

# Capture output — 700 frames + mp4, regenerate with test/Presentation.cs.
# The .gdignore stays tracked: without it the importer swallows every frame.
screenshots/*
!screenshots/.gdignore

# three.js, which is an offline modelling tool for art-src/models/build.mjs and
# not a dependency of the game. `npm install` in that directory restores it; the
# three .glb files it writes to assets/models/ are tracked, because those are the
# actual assets and rebuilding them needs Node.
art-src/models/node_modules/

# Blender's rolling autosave. `.blend` is the source and is tracked; `.blend1` is
# the copy Blender leaves of the *previous* save every time it writes one, so it
# is four megabytes of the last version, regenerated on every save and never
# wanted by anyone.
*.blend1

# Python bytecode from the art-src plate scripts.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Sketchfab "Free Standard" content, which is not CC0 and not CC-BY.
#
# The licence permits using the model in a product, commercially, worldwide, in
# any derivative work — and forbids redistributing the file itself as a
# standalone asset. A raw .glb committed to a public repository is exactly that,
# so the *source* stays out of the history and only the bake goes in.
#
# `assets/models/SOURCE.md` carries the download URL and the sha256, so the file
# is one download away rather than lost. This is the same call the project made
# about `rigged_anime_girl_cc0.blend` and for a licence reason rather than a
# size one.
assets/models/polyart_zombies.glb
assets/models/polyart_zombies.glb.import

# And the textures Godot cuts out of it on import, which this entry did not have
# and the three below it did.
#
# A `.glb` carries its textures inside itself, and the importer writes them out
# beside it as `<name>_<n>.png` the first time it reads the file. So excluding
# the `.glb` excludes the geometry and leaves the artwork sitting in the working
# tree, untracked and unignored, one `git add -A` away from being committed —
# which is the redistribution the licence forbids, minus the mesh.
assets/models/polyart_zombies_*.png
assets/models/polyart_zombies_*.png.import

# A downloaded character whose licence is not recorded, which is a stronger
# reason to keep it out than the size is.
#
# `assets/models/SOURCE.md` has the sha256 and the two blank fields — no page,
# no licence. Every licence that permits shipping the model at all permits the
# bake (vertex data, no texture, no rig), and none of them permit redistributing
# the source file as a standalone asset, which is what this would be. So the
# 3.4 MB source stays out and `resources/bodies/tactical_survivor.res` goes in.
#
# Same call as the Polyart pack above, and as `rigged_anime_girl_cc0.blend`
# before it. Delete this line once SOURCE.md names a licence that allows it.
assets/models/tactical_character.glb
assets/models/tactical_character.glb.import
assets/models/tactical_character_0.jpg
assets/models/tactical_character_0.jpg.import
assets/models/tactical_character_1.jpg
assets/models/tactical_character_1.jpg.import

# RIN, and her ancestor. Same archive, same missing licence — see
# `assets/models/SOURCE.md`. The bake in `resources/bodies/drifter.res` is
# committed; the sources are one file each away from a licence line that would
# let them in.
assets/models/rin_v1_reference.glb
assets/models/rin_v1_reference.glb.import
assets/models/rin_v1_reference_*.png
assets/models/rin_v1_reference_*.png.import
assets/models/rin_v2.glb
assets/models/rin_v2.glb.import
assets/models/rin_v2_*.png
assets/models/rin_v2_*.png.import

# The Blender survivor experiments, which nothing loads.
#
# `build_survivor.py`, `build_survivor_v2.py` and `build_survivor_from_cc0.py`
# each cut a whole survivor out of `art-src/models/base/rigged_anime_girl_cc0.blend`
# and write the export here. All three predate the roster: the game's five
# characters come from PROJECT LAST DAWN and load out of `resources/bodies/`, and
# grep finds no `.cs`, `.tres` or `.tscn` that names any of these files.
#
# So they are outputs kept only so the scripts that made them can be re-run — and
# the largest is a 10 MB re-export of the base blend itself, which is the same ten
# megabytes of unattributable geometry the base file is excluded for. The `.blend`
# sources stay tracked because they are the recipe; the exports do not, because
# re-running the script is the way to get one back.
assets/models/survivor_anime_apocalypse.glb
assets/models/survivor_anime_apocalypse.glb.import
assets/models/survivor_anime_apocalypse_*.webp
assets/models/survivor_anime_apocalypse_*.webp.import

# The same, for the two `survivor_apocalypse` exports: the importer's extracted
# textures and its sidecars. The `.glb` files themselves are tracked from before
# this rule and are the entry above them in `README.md`'s ledger, not here.
assets/models/survivor_apocalypse*.glb.import
assets/models/survivor_apocalypse_*.png
assets/models/survivor_apocalypse_*.png.import
assets/textures/survivor_material_atlas_v2.png
assets/textures/survivor_material_atlas_v2.png.import

# PROJECT LAST DAWN's five characters. **CC BY 4.0 permits committing these** —
# see `assets/models/SOURCE.md`, which finally has an author, a URL and a licence
# after three phases of blanks. They stay out for size alone: five LOD1 exports
# are 47 MB, against 11 MB for the five bakes that are committed and are what the
# game actually loads. `art-src/models/intake.ps1` rebuilds a bake from one in a
# single command.
assets/models/ld_*.glb
assets/models/ld_*.glb.import
assets/models/ld_*.png
assets/models/ld_*.png.import
assets/models/ld_*.jpg
assets/models/ld_*.jpg.import
assets/models/chr_*.glb
assets/models/chr_*.glb.import
assets/models/chr_*.png
assets/models/chr_*.png.import
assets/models/chr_*.jpg
assets/models/chr_*.jpg.import
